1
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:04,280
A luxury hotel turned smoky deathtrap.

2
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:06,920
Soviet casualties of the space race.

3
00:00:07,540 --> 00:00:10,140
A plane that drops mysteriously from the sky.

4
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:12,780
A car that ignites on impact.

5
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:14,820
Tragic blunders.

6
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:18,260
Or inevitable missteps on the path to progress.

7
00:00:19,580 --> 00:00:23,880
Now, Engineering Disasters 4 on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
From Egyptian pyramids, to medieval castles, to 21st century skylines,

9
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:48,180
the world has been shaped by engineering feats of dazzling invention.

10
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:50,780
But progress comes with a price.

11
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:57,200
When exploring the unknown, disasters are often an inevitable and valuable prelude to success.

12
00:00:57,201 --> 00:01:09,840
The entire process of design theory development has come to a great extent from lessons experienced through trial and error.

13
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:18,720
Few modern venues are as well suited to calculate the odds of success than the glittering hotel casinos of Las Vegas.

14
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:26,640
So when local firefighters received an alarm from the MGM Grand Hotel on November 21st, 1980,

15
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,700
they figured the call was routine.

16
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:33,960
It has fire protection systems in place, and it's a brand new building.

17
00:01:33,961 --> 00:01:37,240
So you're kind of bored by the whole affair.

18
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:41,780
We made a right-hand turn where we could actually see the MGM,

19
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,540
and it was like an A-bomb went off.

20
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:49,180
As more fire companies were called to the scene,

21
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:52,700
the blaze at the ground floor casino was roaring out of control.

22
00:01:52,701 --> 00:01:57,980
Hundreds of hotel guests were trapped on the floors above by rising smoke

23
00:01:57,981 --> 00:02:04,000
You could see people up there 100 yards away and they were in fear of their lives

24
00:02:04,001 --> 00:02:08,740
A lot of them had very little clothes or whatever they had with them

25
00:02:08,741 --> 00:02:10,480
Some of them had towels over their face

26
00:02:10,481 --> 00:02:13,380
You could see a lot of them had, you know, taken in some smoke

27
00:02:13,381 --> 00:02:16,040
So they were definitely running for their lives

28
00:02:16,041 --> 00:02:20,340
But the MGM fire was a tragedy that could have been avoided

29
00:02:20,341 --> 00:02:28,080
It was a disaster whose genesis began with flawed engineering and was later compounded by poor maintenance and bad luck.

30
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:38,560
When the MGM opened on the Vegas Strip in 1972, fire codes called for sprinkler systems throughout the 27-story building and T-shaped guest tower.

31
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:45,800
But an exception was made for the MGM's ground floor casino and an adjoining deli restaurant.

32
00:02:45,801 --> 00:02:54,460
Since both were open 24 hours a day, it was decided that any fire that broke out in those areas could be quickly contained

33
00:02:54,461 --> 00:03:00,960
Sometime after the hotel opened, however, the casino's deli restaurant began closing at midnight

34
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:10,860
Early on the morning of November 21st, a fire started in an enclosed area of the restaurant that was used for busing dishes, known as a side station

35
00:03:10,861 --> 00:03:16,380
Investigators built this full-scale reproduction of the side station

36
00:03:16,381 --> 00:03:19,400
to trace the movement of the fire and film the blaze.

37
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:27,120
The delicatessen side station had a two-foot by two-foot air transfer grill

38
00:03:27,121 --> 00:03:29,040
located in its ceiling.

39
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:36,200
As a result of this, the fire could develop in that space undetected

40
00:03:36,201 --> 00:03:40,380
and developed to the point that we know as flashover,

41
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:43,360
which makes it much more hazardous.

42
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:48,440
Flashover is the point at which virtually everything that's flammable

43
00:03:48,441 --> 00:03:50,660
within an enclosed area starts to burn.

44
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:55,160
Once the fire reached flashover in the deli restaurant,

45
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:56,860
it roared into the casino,

46
00:03:57,260 --> 00:03:59,600
which was filled with combustible furnishings,

47
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:02,580
12 tons of glue in the ceiling tiles alone.

48
00:04:02,581 --> 00:04:12,000
Meanwhile, a flawed air control system allowed smoke and carbon monoxide to sift upwards toward the top levels of the guest towers.

49
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:20,340
There were a number of shafts within the building, the elevator shafts, the stair shafts, in addition to the ventilation system.

50
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:26,120
And so it was people actually on the uppermost floors that were subjected to the most severe smoke conditions.

51
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:32,260
The interior exit stairwells became the most dangerous places of all.

52
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:35,160
escape routes that turned into death traps.

53
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,320
There was smoke coming up some of the stairways,

54
00:04:40,040 --> 00:04:44,160
and when people tried to leave the stairways because of the smoke,

55
00:04:44,620 --> 00:04:49,120
they found that the doors from the stairway side had been locked for security purposes,

56
00:04:50,020 --> 00:04:53,380
and they couldn't get out of the stairways on any floor.

57
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:58,220
One firefighting device in the hotel worked perfectly.

58
00:04:58,840 --> 00:04:59,980
The sprinkler system.

59
00:04:59,981 --> 00:05:06,120
The areas without sprinklers, the casino and deli, became charred ruins.

60
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:09,200
But sprinklers kept the fire from spreading elsewhere.

61
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:12,400
Anywhere there were sprinklers, the fire stopped in its tracks.

62
00:05:12,401 --> 00:05:17,460
The sprinklers themselves put it out without any help from firefighters or whatever.

63
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:18,500
It went out right there.

64
00:05:18,580 --> 00:05:21,880
That's like the untold story and the whole affair.

65
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:28,520
If that building didn't have a sprinkler system, it would have burned from top to bottom, side to side.

66
00:05:28,521 --> 00:05:30,260
the whole building would have burned.

67
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:35,360
The MGM Grand Fire ultimately claimed 85 lives,

68
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:38,320
the second most deadly hotel fire in American history.

69
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:42,460
The tragedy sparked a massive reform of fire protection systems,

70
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:43,840
starting in Las Vegas.

71
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:46,040
Control to Unit 6.

72
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:48,620
We're spawned down to Central Plant.

73
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:50,780
We've got a trouble with the smoke detector down there.

74
00:05:52,380 --> 00:05:56,280
Today, Bally's Hotel stands on the site of the former MGM Grand.

75
00:05:57,220 --> 00:06:01,220
It boasts a state-of-the-art system designed to foil any fire in its tracks,

76
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:03,700
starting at the fireproof command center.

77
00:06:04,580 --> 00:06:07,380
This system monitors over 12,000 points or valleys.

78
00:06:07,381 --> 00:06:10,620
We have over 1,800 smoke detectors throughout the hotel.

79
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:14,380
We also monitor all the sprinkler flow systems,

80
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:18,180
also any control valve associated with the sprinkler system.

81
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:22,140
If a fire signal comes to the command center,

82
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:26,360
elevators can be automatically shut off or rerouted to safe areas.

83
00:06:28,180 --> 00:06:31,900
Phone jacks at all floors allow instant communication with firefighters.

84
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:36,780
And interior stairwells, the notorious fire traps in the MGM Grand Blaze,

85
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:42,100
are pressurized and equipped with air blowers to create smoke-free zones for escape.

86
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:45,300
Stairwells and the towers are going to pressurize.

87
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:50,360
That keeps smoke out so people can get in to the hallways, get into the stairwells,

88
00:06:50,361 --> 00:06:55,040
and that's an area of refuge that they can use to get down to the ground level and get out of the building.

89
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,600
Sprinklers are installed at 10-foot intervals or less throughout the hotel,

90
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:03,400
including the cavernous casino at Ground Zero.

91
00:07:07,060 --> 00:07:10,780
The sprinklers are a reminder that even in modern high-rise buildings,

92
00:07:11,380 --> 00:07:14,540
most fires are best contained when water is easily at hand.

93
00:07:14,541 --> 00:07:24,140
Between 20 to 24 gallons per minute will come out of these heads, and that's quite a bit of water in anybody's terms.

94
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:26,780
It'll put a serious hurt on a fire.

95
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:33,660
Despite the engineering flaw of omitting sprinkler systems in the MGM's casino and restaurant,

96
00:07:33,940 --> 00:07:36,940
that fire might still have been contained if detected sooner.

97
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:44,240
But the 1987 collapse of the partially constructed L'Ambiance Plaza Towers in Bridgeport, Connecticut,

98
00:07:44,540 --> 00:07:50,500
An avalanche of concrete and steel which killed 28 construction workers unfolded in an instant.

99
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:56,480
This was a collapse that happened without any warning.

100
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:02,840
The buildings just took off and crumbled.

101
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:10,440
Designed in the early 1980s as two connecting 16-story towers,

102
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:15,500
L'Ambiance Plaza was intended to provide affordable housing in downtown Bridgeport.

103
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:23,100
To set the floors in place, engineers proposed using the popular lift slab technique,

104
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:27,040
a method prized by builders for both economy and safety.

105
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:35,420
The advantage of the method from a safety point of view is all of the concrete work is being done at the ground level

106
00:08:35,421 --> 00:08:37,440
rather than working high in the building.

107
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:48,500
To pull the slabs up from the ground, steel collars called shear heads frame the openings in the floor slabs around the steel columns.

108
00:08:49,980 --> 00:08:59,200
The slabs are then hoisted by lifting rods, each of which is attached to a large nut that fits under a component of the shear heads, known as the lifting angle.

109
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:09,180
The slabs are then jacked up to resting locations, where they are supported by temporary wedges, tack welded into the columns.

110
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:14,740
The vertical pressure exerted by the weight of the slabs keeps the nut and the wedges in place.

111
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:20,360
Those wedges are nothing else but loosely put in wedges.

112
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:22,920
They are not held by anything else.

113
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:23,740
Friction.

114
00:09:23,741 --> 00:09:29,960
The assumption is that that nut is going to sit there transferring the vertical load

115
00:09:29,961 --> 00:09:32,420
and is not going to shift horizontally.

116
00:09:32,421 --> 00:09:41,440
Well, that assumption apparently is not as reliable as people have thought.

117
00:09:42,780 --> 00:09:48,700
The 2,900-ton slabs at L'Ambiance were hoisted three at a time from ground level.

118
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:55,040
Investigators believe that the accident began when a lifting angle in one of the shear heads deformed,

119
00:09:55,740 --> 00:10:00,860
causing the nut underneath to slide out, instantly destabilizing the lift slab operation.

120
00:10:00,861 --> 00:10:07,280
that the nut has slipped out, and then we are unsupported.

121
00:10:07,760 --> 00:10:10,040
More weight is dumped to the other columns.

122
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:14,140
The slab starts breaking, and the entire cell collapses.

123
00:10:18,380 --> 00:10:21,220
Deficient welds used in the construction of the shear heads

124
00:10:21,221 --> 00:10:23,580
may have eroded support for the slabs as well.

125
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:29,540
Once the slabs began falling, the weight of three concrete units,

126
00:10:29,541 --> 00:10:36,540
This, close to 9,000 tons, created a pancake effect on the floors below.

127
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:44,540
The lift slab technique also depends on the stability of its surrounding structure.

128
00:10:44,541 --> 00:10:48,800
But when the accident at L'Ambiance Plaza occurred, construction of those walls lagged

129
00:10:48,801 --> 00:10:53,540
several stories below the area where the slabs were being raised.

130
00:10:53,541 --> 00:10:57,360
Without the presence of surrounding walls to provide lateral stability,

131
00:10:58,140 --> 00:11:01,840
a localized accident became a scene of massive destruction.

132
00:11:03,220 --> 00:11:09,260
When the failure occurred, there were many, many, many thousands of tons of concrete

133
00:11:09,261 --> 00:11:13,060
raining down to the ground in a very short time.

134
00:11:13,061 --> 00:11:16,480
So that was, of course, very catastrophic.

135
00:11:18,180 --> 00:11:23,280
The totality of the L'Ambiance Plaza collapse made it difficult for investigators to pinpoint

136
00:11:23,281 --> 00:11:25,460
the triggering cause with absolute certainty.

137
00:11:28,600 --> 00:11:30,300
But following the disaster,

138
00:11:30,780 --> 00:11:33,300
the state of Connecticut banned lift slab construction

139
00:11:33,301 --> 00:11:35,060
on all high-rise buildings.

140
00:11:37,460 --> 00:11:39,400
While it remains legal elsewhere,

141
00:11:40,040 --> 00:11:42,740
the lift slab method has been almost entirely abandoned,

142
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:46,600
underscoring the magnitude of the tragedy at L'Ambiance Plaza.

143
00:11:47,780 --> 00:11:49,620
28 people died there,

144
00:11:50,240 --> 00:11:52,820
making it one of the most fatal construction accidents

145
00:11:52,821 --> 00:11:54,100
in American history.

146
00:11:57,860 --> 00:12:00,800
The three fire pumps at Bally's Las Vegas Hotel

147
00:12:00,801 --> 00:12:02,720
are each capable of ejecting water

148
00:12:02,721 --> 00:12:05,240
at a rate of 1,500 gallons per minute.

149
00:12:06,860 --> 00:12:09,980
Engineering Disasters 4 will return on Modern Marvelous.

150
00:12:13,880 --> 00:12:17,980
In 1957, the Soviet Union successfully launched

151
00:12:17,981 --> 00:12:19,940
its Sputnik into Earth orbit.

152
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:27,540
The satellite's success turned outer space into a Cold War battleground between the Soviet Union and the United States.

153
00:12:28,140 --> 00:12:31,920
The space race really was an offshoot of military need.

154
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:36,680
I mean, both the rocket that launched Sputnik was really initially an ICBM.

155
00:12:36,780 --> 00:12:40,480
It was designed to bring, send a nuclear bomb to the United States.

156
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:46,540
But for the Soviet Union, the high-stakes race to gain the upper hand in military might

157
00:12:46,541 --> 00:12:49,660
would lead to the most fatal rocket disaster in history.

158
00:12:49,940 --> 00:12:54,227
By the fall of 1960, presidential candidate John F.

159
00:12:54,228 --> 00:13:00,280
Kennedy was charging that America was on the short end of a missile gap.

160
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:08,580
At the same time, Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev was boasting of his own country's overwhelming missile strength.

161
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:12,940
In reality, the Soviets lagged far behind.

162
00:13:12,941 --> 00:13:19,480
Khrushchev gave a speech in which he said that we're building these rockets like sausages.

163
00:13:19,980 --> 00:13:23,700
So the West believes the Russians have a lot of ICBMs.

164
00:13:23,701 --> 00:13:25,520
Well, in truth, they really didn't.

165
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:29,680
They had this really poor ICBM called the R-7, which didn't work very well.

166
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:33,880
So they were really hurriedly trying to develop a second one called the R-16.

167
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:42,280
As the Soviets prepared for the test launch of their two-stage R-16 missile in October of 1960,

168
00:13:42,900 --> 00:13:44,460
Khrushchev ratcheted up the pressure.

169
00:13:46,540 --> 00:13:49,700
He dispatched his chief designer, Mikhail Yangl,

170
00:13:50,340 --> 00:13:55,620
and his missile troop commander, Field Marshal Mitrofan Nedelin, to the launch site,

171
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,380
adding political pressure to an already tense and uncertain operation.

172
00:14:00,140 --> 00:14:02,760
This is about the Cold War. We need this missile.

173
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:08,860
So this sort of pressure goes down from every level, down from Khrushchev, down to Nedelin, the commander,

174
00:14:08,861 --> 00:14:12,460
down to the chief designer, Yangel, down to his engineers, down to the soldiers.

175
00:14:12,840 --> 00:14:14,500
Everybody knows this is a big deal.

176
00:14:15,660 --> 00:14:17,680
Initially set for October 23rd,

177
00:14:18,200 --> 00:14:22,800
the R-16 launch was delayed when major fuel leaks were discovered in the two-stage rocket.

178
00:14:24,440 --> 00:14:27,200
But instead of following standard safety procedures,

179
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:31,540
Field Marshal Nadehlin ordered the rockets repaired without draining the fuel tanks,

180
00:14:32,140 --> 00:14:34,140
a calculated gamble to save time.

181
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,660
Engineers were still working on the rocket the next day,

182
00:14:38,860 --> 00:14:40,900
as the countdown began.

183
00:14:40,901 --> 00:14:44,100
About 30 minutes before the scheduled 9 p.m. launch,

184
00:14:44,101 --> 00:14:49,900
a spurious communication signal prematurely activated the rocket's second stage.

185
00:14:49,901 --> 00:14:53,240
The engine ignites, the fire goes into the first stage,

186
00:14:53,241 --> 00:14:56,960
which is packed with toxic propellants.

187
00:14:56,961 --> 00:14:58,660
The first stage explodes.

188
00:14:58,661 --> 00:15:01,860
Immediately there's a gigantic fireball.

189
00:15:01,861 --> 00:15:06,620
Hundreds of tons of toxic propellant have just exploded.

190
00:15:06,621 --> 00:15:09,740
A camera set up to record the launch captured the horrific scene.

191
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:18,560
As hundreds fled for their lives, those standing close to the rocket, including Field Marshal Nedalen, were instantly incinerated.

192
00:15:19,220 --> 00:15:26,340
At its apex, the temperature of the fire reached 3,000 degrees, melting everything in its path.

193
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:32,240
Over 150 people died, including many top Soviet scientists and engineers.

194
00:15:32,840 --> 00:15:37,360
But for decades afterwards, the story of the disaster remained hidden from the public.

195
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:41,960
It wasn't even acceptable to admit that this happened.

196
00:15:41,961 --> 00:15:44,920
You would be violating state secrecies.

197
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:49,460
So people who were mourning for their relatives couldn't officially, openly mourn.

198
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:53,100
Six months after the R-16 disaster,

199
00:15:53,740 --> 00:15:57,740
cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin became the first man to successfully orbit the Earth.

200
00:15:57,741 --> 00:16:05,460
To all outward appearances, the Soviets were capturing the high ground of space.

201
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,700
Yuri Gagarin goes up into space in April 1961.

202
00:16:10,020 --> 00:16:15,840
He's the first human being in space, which is, of course, a great accomplishment.

203
00:16:16,600 --> 00:16:21,320
But, you know, it wasn't balanced by the tragedy of six months prior.

204
00:16:21,321 --> 00:16:29,140
Responding to Gagarin's success, President Kennedy issued a challenge of his own, setting

205
00:16:29,141 --> 00:16:33,200
into motion a space race that would result in one of America's greatest technological

206
00:16:33,201 --> 00:16:34,320
triumphs.

207
00:16:34,321 --> 00:16:41,320
This nation should commit itself to achieving the goal, before this decade is out, of landing

208
00:16:41,321 --> 00:16:44,380
a man on the moon and returning him safely to the earth.

209
00:16:44,381 --> 00:16:56,678
As the United States made step-by-step progress toward that goal, marking well-publicized failures as well as triumphs, the Soviets embarked on a clandestine program

210
00:16:56,679 --> 00:16:58,020
to reach the moon.

211
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:06,520
But inferior technology and official secrecy doomed their bid to compete.

212
00:17:06,521 --> 00:17:11,500
part of the problem of Soviet Union

213
00:17:11,501 --> 00:17:14,340
that they tried to get to the moon

214
00:17:14,341 --> 00:17:16,580
with a hand file and a hacksaw.

215
00:17:18,140 --> 00:17:19,800
The other aspect

216
00:17:19,801 --> 00:17:23,380
was the pressure from politicians

217
00:17:23,381 --> 00:17:27,200
and the level of secrecy that that system required.

218
00:17:28,480 --> 00:17:30,620
What the Soviet system produced

219
00:17:30,621 --> 00:17:31,920
was the N-1,

220
00:17:32,460 --> 00:17:34,120
an enormous three-stage rocket

221
00:17:34,121 --> 00:17:35,980
330 feet tall

222
00:17:35,981 --> 00:17:38,040
and propelled by 30 engines.

223
00:17:39,640 --> 00:17:42,020
It's so big that American spy satellites

224
00:17:42,021 --> 00:17:44,020
can see it very nicely on the pad.

225
00:17:44,260 --> 00:17:45,280
They know that it exists,

226
00:17:45,880 --> 00:17:48,620
even though the Soviets haven't publicly announced all this.

227
00:17:51,460 --> 00:17:53,400
In the summer of 1969,

228
00:17:53,960 --> 00:17:56,140
as the U.S. prepared to launch Neil Armstrong,

229
00:17:56,580 --> 00:17:58,700
Buzz Aldrin, and Michael Collins to the moon,

230
00:18:00,080 --> 00:18:02,840
the Soviets were still hoping to stay in the race

231
00:18:02,841 --> 00:18:05,540
by sending an unmanned N-1 rocket on a moon mission.

232
00:18:06,940 --> 00:18:09,360
Their whole plan was, we're going to fly the Superbooster.

233
00:18:09,820 --> 00:18:11,180
It's going to go around the moon.

234
00:18:11,360 --> 00:18:14,240
It's not going to have a crew on board, but there is a lot of anticipation.

235
00:18:14,580 --> 00:18:15,720
It's like a real race.

236
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:22,780
On July 3rd, 1969, the N-1 took off as scheduled from the launch pad.

237
00:18:23,580 --> 00:18:27,900
But just after liftoff, a misfiring engine set off a chain reaction

238
00:18:27,901 --> 00:18:30,260
that sent the rocket to a fiery doom.

239
00:18:31,420 --> 00:18:33,800
The thrust is mismatched.

240
00:18:33,801 --> 00:18:36,620
the rocket starts to swerve a little bit,

241
00:18:36,980 --> 00:18:39,900
and it's only raised a few hundred feet above the pad.

242
00:18:40,320 --> 00:18:43,740
And eventually, the computer command basically shuts down all the engines.

243
00:18:44,180 --> 00:18:47,920
The rocket, of course, slowly comes back down on the pad,

244
00:18:48,280 --> 00:18:49,800
just explodes in a massive explosion.

245
00:18:50,580 --> 00:18:55,560
And it's probably the biggest explosion ever in the history of rocket.

246
00:18:58,260 --> 00:19:01,120
Although no one was hurt in the N-1 rocket disaster,

247
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:05,040
the Soviets made just two more failed attempts to launch the rocket

248
00:19:05,041 --> 00:19:07,320
before abandoning the N1 mission for good.

249
00:19:09,200 --> 00:19:14,180
It was a program at once propelled and doomed by the secretive system that surrounded it.

250
00:19:14,940 --> 00:19:17,840
There wasn't much exchange within the Soviet Union.

251
00:19:18,220 --> 00:19:21,000
The failures that they had were not discussed.

252
00:19:21,001 --> 00:19:25,160
You discuss the failure, you were accused of treason.

253
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:29,960
As the Soviet Empire began to crumble in 1989,

254
00:19:30,840 --> 00:19:35,160
Details of the N-1 rocket and R-16 missile failures finally came to light.

255
00:19:36,320 --> 00:19:40,260
By that time, the Soviet space station Mir was well established.

256
00:19:42,500 --> 00:19:45,420
One could say that the failures of the moon program

257
00:19:45,421 --> 00:19:48,140
eventually led them to this space station path,

258
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:51,220
which became Salute, then became Mir,

259
00:19:51,221 --> 00:19:54,440
and then eventually is now the International Space Station.

260
00:19:55,340 --> 00:19:58,400
But if Mir was a triumph of Soviet technology,

261
00:19:58,401 --> 00:20:05,400
Its development was due in part to the abandoned schemes of decades past.

262
00:20:05,401 --> 00:20:10,400
The first successful R-16 missile was launched in 1961.

263
00:20:10,401 --> 00:20:15,400
By 1967, there were 197 R-16 missiles in the Soviet arsenal,

264
00:20:15,401 --> 00:20:21,400
each equipped to carry a 3 to 6 megaton nuclear bomb.

265
00:20:21,401 --> 00:20:27,400
Engineering Disasters 4 will return on Modern Marvels.

266
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:35,920
The Point Pleasant Bridge, which spanned the border between southern Ohio and West Virginia,

267
00:20:36,260 --> 00:20:41,360
was filled with commuters and Christmas shoppers on the afternoon of December 16, 1967.

268
00:20:42,580 --> 00:20:49,160
Suddenly, the bridge collapsed, sending dozens of cars and passengers into the frigid waters of the Ohio River.

269
00:20:50,020 --> 00:20:56,160
The death toll would ultimately reach 46, the worst loss of life from a bridge accident in American history.

270
00:20:57,100 --> 00:21:02,780
The bridge was built in 1928, and it came down in 1967.

271
00:21:03,220 --> 00:21:08,820
One has to ask, how could a bridge exist for almost 40 years and then all of a sudden collapse?

272
00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:16,460
The mysterious collapse of the Point Pleasant Bridge can be traced to its unusual construction nearly four decades earlier.

273
00:21:18,620 --> 00:21:24,220
Unlike classic suspension bridges like the Golden Gate, which are linked by hundreds of wound steel cables,

274
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:30,880
Point Pleasant's construction was of a type similar to Pittsburgh's 6th Street Bridge,

275
00:21:31,540 --> 00:21:33,620
which was constructed at the same time,

276
00:21:34,300 --> 00:21:38,520
and whose suspension depended on parallel chains of steel eyebars.

277
00:21:40,520 --> 00:21:45,460
Each end of the eyebar are holes through which pins are passed,

278
00:21:45,560 --> 00:21:50,200
so it's a series of straight links that are pinned to articulate

279
00:21:50,201 --> 00:21:54,800
and achieve more or less the curve of a suspension bridge.

280
00:21:54,801 --> 00:21:57,340
was very strong material.

281
00:21:57,980 --> 00:22:02,160
This allowed them to build the bridge in a very light fashion

282
00:22:02,161 --> 00:22:06,000
so that they could have long, lightweight elements in the bridge.

283
00:22:08,040 --> 00:22:10,260
Coated with shiny aluminum paint,

284
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:13,300
Point Pleasant became known as the Silver Bridge,

285
00:22:13,301 --> 00:22:18,440
an elegant and aesthetically pleasing structure 1,460 feet long.

286
00:22:19,780 --> 00:22:23,080
But unlike the cables of a traditional suspension bridge,

287
00:22:24,040 --> 00:22:27,420
The silver bridge eyebars provided no redundant means of support.

288
00:22:29,040 --> 00:22:34,000
Instead, they formed a chain, which proved to be only as strong as its weakest link.

289
00:22:34,920 --> 00:22:39,660
If one significant part fails, the entire bridge will collapse.

290
00:22:40,100 --> 00:22:48,660
I don't know when that concept became well known, but it must not have been practiced in the 1920s.

291
00:22:48,661 --> 00:22:49,361
1920s.

292
00:22:49,362 --> 00:22:56,020
After examining the wreckage from the Ohio River, a team from the National Bureau of Standards,

293
00:22:56,600 --> 00:23:01,780
now known as the National Institute of Standards and Technology, created this model of the Silver

294
00:23:01,781 --> 00:23:02,481
Bridge.

295
00:23:02,482 --> 00:23:08,040
They determined that the fatal fissure occurred at one end of I-bar number 330, the

296
00:23:08,041 --> 00:23:14,747
result of a one-eighth inch crack caused by decades of corrosion.

297
00:23:14,748 --> 00:23:17,740
When the I-bar finally broke,

298
00:23:18,660 --> 00:23:23,040
The force of the fracture loosened the connecting pin in the joint where the break occurred,

299
00:23:23,780 --> 00:23:27,180
allowing the other eye bars to slip out,

300
00:23:28,360 --> 00:23:29,760
breaking the chain of suspension,

301
00:23:31,100 --> 00:23:32,180
dropping the roadbed,

302
00:23:32,860 --> 00:23:34,320
and collapsing the bridge.

303
00:23:35,500 --> 00:23:38,780
This is where the brittle fracture occurred.

304
00:23:38,900 --> 00:23:42,260
Actually, it's been removed for microscopic examination,

305
00:23:42,620 --> 00:23:45,260
but it was very flat, just like this.

306
00:23:45,261 --> 00:23:51,740
the silver bridge eye bars were made of heat treated medium high carbon steel

307
00:23:51,741 --> 00:23:57,920
an unusually strong but brittle metal that is susceptible to cracking from natural corrosion

308
00:23:57,921 --> 00:24:03,900
but the covered joints where the fatal fracture occurred were concealed from view during routine

309
00:24:03,901 --> 00:24:13,480
inspections of the bridge the last real inspection was back in 1951 apparently this was done by

310
00:24:13,481 --> 00:24:16,620
someone looking at the bridge with binoculars from the ground.

311
00:24:17,080 --> 00:24:20,280
So again, we have a maintenance problem for sure.

312
00:24:22,380 --> 00:24:26,540
The collapse of the Silver Bridge led to a systematic inspection of bridges

313
00:24:26,541 --> 00:24:29,240
throughout the country for the first time in U.S. history.

314
00:24:31,820 --> 00:24:36,940
Many older bridges were either dismantled or retrofitted to meet new safety guidelines.

315
00:24:36,941 --> 00:24:46,600
There was a general looking at bridges to examine their redundancy and their susceptibility to this kind of failure.

316
00:24:47,100 --> 00:24:53,860
It certainly had an impact on all future bridges that were built, so they never built one like this again.

317
00:24:57,360 --> 00:25:03,020
Oversights and maintenance procedures were also to blame in the collapse of the world's first floating concrete bridge.

318
00:25:03,021 --> 00:25:05,835
Built in 1940 in Seattle, the Lacey V.

319
00:25:05,836 --> 00:25:12,800
Murrow Bridge was named after the director of Washington State's Department of Transportation.

320
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:22,380
The bridge stood for 50 years, but in November of 1990, a rehabilitation and maintenance operation on the bridge led to disaster.

321
00:25:22,381 --> 00:25:32,640
Spanning a short but deep section of Seattle's Lake Washington,

322
00:25:33,440 --> 00:25:40,340
the Murrow Bridge floated on a row of 25 hollow concrete pontoons, each 350 feet long.

323
00:25:41,580 --> 00:25:46,780
The pontoons were bolted together for stability and anchored to the bottom of the lake.

324
00:25:46,781 --> 00:25:54,120
The conditions are just right for use of such a structure

325
00:25:54,121 --> 00:26:00,980
because the water conditions under which they're installed are perfect.

326
00:26:00,981 --> 00:26:08,180
That is, very seldom is there any ice, very seldom is there a heavy current,

327
00:26:08,400 --> 00:26:12,080
and the water is very deep, 200 or more feet.

328
00:26:12,081 --> 00:26:20,900
In the fall of 1990, the Murrow Bridge was undergoing a facelift by means of hydro demolition,

329
00:26:20,901 --> 00:26:25,480
a process in which highly pressurized water is used to break up the roadway.

330
00:26:27,360 --> 00:26:32,020
Since environmental regulations prohibited dumping the contaminated water into the lake,

331
00:26:32,860 --> 00:26:40,200
it was temporarily stored in the empty pontoons via access holes which were cut into the tops and sides of some sections.

332
00:26:41,160 --> 00:26:46,560
The water level inside the pontoons was carefully monitored, so the structure would not be compromised.

333
00:26:50,640 --> 00:26:56,480
But on Thanksgiving weekend, with bridge crews shorthanded, a heavy storm hit the Seattle area.

334
00:26:57,980 --> 00:27:00,340
Rainwater drained into the open pontoons.

335
00:27:01,160 --> 00:27:06,500
Winds from the storm whipped up the surface of the lake, propelling splash water into the pontoons as well.

336
00:27:06,501 --> 00:27:13,040
On the morning of November 25th, eight waterlogged pontoons sunk into the lake,

337
00:27:13,780 --> 00:27:16,080
taking those sections of the bridge down with them

338
00:27:16,081 --> 00:27:20,720
and severing 12 anchoring cables of a bridge that was being constructed nearby.

339
00:27:21,360 --> 00:27:26,120
While luckily no one was injured, the cost of damage was estimated at $69 million.

340
00:27:26,780 --> 00:27:31,540
An investigation of the accident came to a sobering, if unsurprising, conclusion.

341
00:27:31,541 --> 00:27:39,280
After a very extensive study and very sophisticated computer modeling methods,

342
00:27:39,460 --> 00:27:46,360
the conclusion was that concrete pontoons simply don't float when they're full of water.

343
00:27:47,060 --> 00:27:49,460
A very unsophisticated mode of failure.

344
00:27:51,700 --> 00:27:55,360
The Lacey v. Murrow Bridge was rebuilt two years later.

345
00:27:55,780 --> 00:27:57,520
Though it looks the same from the outside,

346
00:27:57,521 --> 00:28:01,480
the pontoons that support the bridge have been divided into smaller sections

347
00:28:01,481 --> 00:28:03,380
to minimize damage from leaks,

348
00:28:03,740 --> 00:28:06,280
a precaution that underscores the structural similarities

349
00:28:06,281 --> 00:28:09,080
between floating bridges and floating ships.

350
00:28:09,920 --> 00:28:13,240
The pontoons were designed with multiple cells,

351
00:28:13,241 --> 00:28:17,380
not unlike the multiple cell supertankers,

352
00:28:17,700 --> 00:28:22,300
so that failure in one place would not bring down the entire pontoon.

353
00:28:23,340 --> 00:28:26,520
What has happened here, they simply had a ship

354
00:28:26,521 --> 00:28:29,580
in which they made a hole in the hole,

355
00:28:29,940 --> 00:28:31,060
and they put it in the water.

356
00:28:31,061 --> 00:28:34,840
Well, the ship filled with water and sank.

357
00:28:35,520 --> 00:28:37,140
That's what the bridge was all about.

358
00:28:38,600 --> 00:28:42,680
The Lacey V. Murrow bridge collapse caused no deaths or injuries,

359
00:28:43,100 --> 00:28:49,480
but it provided a dramatic reminder that no engineering feat remains entirely immune from the forces of nature.

360
00:28:52,300 --> 00:28:56,940
Lacey V. Murrow was the brother of legendary broadcaster Edward R. Murrow.

361
00:28:56,941 --> 00:29:00,940
Engineering Disasters 4 will return on Modern Marvels.

362
00:29:04,940 --> 00:29:09,040
Why I spent an inordinate amount of time on the Corvair,

363
00:29:09,760 --> 00:29:13,500
it is because the Corvair is an inordinately dangerous vehicle.

364
00:29:14,620 --> 00:29:21,220
In the summer of 1966, a Senate subcommittee chaired by Robert Kennedy and Abraham Ribikoff

365
00:29:21,221 --> 00:29:24,020
held hearings on the need for auto safety regulation.

366
00:29:24,021 --> 00:29:33,260
Among those testifying was a young attorney named Ralph Nader, who took dead aim at a popular compact car called the Corvair.

367
00:29:34,680 --> 00:29:43,680
Introduced in September of 1959, the Chevrolet Corvair was the first air-cooled rear-engine automobile mass-manufactured in the United States.

368
00:29:44,480 --> 00:29:46,560
The car was a hit with the public.

369
00:29:47,180 --> 00:29:50,060
Sleek and sporty with an innovative unit body construction,

370
00:29:50,840 --> 00:29:54,960
The Corvair seemed a perfect match for the effervescent mood of the emerging 60s.

371
00:29:57,120 --> 00:29:58,880
It was sort of a happy time.

372
00:29:59,100 --> 00:30:03,700
There was a youth market now that had a craving to have their own cars as well.

373
00:30:04,700 --> 00:30:06,900
It's way ahead of its time for many reasons.

374
00:30:06,901 --> 00:30:10,640
It's a rear-engine car, which was a first with an air-cooled engine

375
00:30:10,641 --> 00:30:14,900
in the guise or genre of Porsche automobiles.

376
00:30:15,640 --> 00:30:18,380
Even to this day, it had outstanding styling.

377
00:30:20,060 --> 00:30:25,340
But the Corvair's rear engine contributed to a weight disparity between the front and rear ends of the car,

378
00:30:25,960 --> 00:30:29,800
making the Corvair unpredictable to control in sharp or sudden turns.

379
00:30:31,500 --> 00:30:35,040
The rear end of the car would swing out to its left,

380
00:30:35,440 --> 00:30:38,880
and the car would want to be making a tighter circle, in other words,

381
00:30:38,881 --> 00:30:43,260
than what the driver was intending and even what the driver was perceiving.

382
00:30:43,820 --> 00:30:49,920
And suddenly the driver would recognize, oh my God, the rear end of the car is swinging out,

383
00:30:50,060 --> 00:30:56,800
and the person driving the car would hit the brakes, which would make the situation worse.

384
00:30:59,400 --> 00:31:06,780
To correct the steering problem, the Corvair owner's manual specified radically different tire pressures for the front and rear wheels.

385
00:31:07,620 --> 00:31:12,940
26 to 28 pounds per square inch in front and just 15 PSI in the rear.

386
00:31:14,300 --> 00:31:16,800
It was advice that many owners routinely overlooked.

387
00:31:16,801 --> 00:31:17,556
overlooked.

388
00:31:17,557 --> 00:31:22,920
But critics like Nader claimed the Corvair's control problems were exacerbated

389
00:31:22,921 --> 00:31:29,100
by the car's swing axle rear suspension. During sharp cornering, it was alleged that

390
00:31:29,101 --> 00:31:34,460
the vertical angle of the rear wheel, or camber, would tuck under so sharply that the car was

391
00:31:34,461 --> 00:31:36,018
prone to a rollover.

392
00:31:36,019 --> 00:31:41,080
The tucking under of one of the rear wheels, then the steel wheel

393
00:31:41,081 --> 00:31:44,560
rim would gouge into the pavement and would serve as a tripping mechanism.

394
00:31:45,780 --> 00:31:51,000
The publicity surrounding the Kennedy-Ribikoff Senate hearings helped shift popular opinion

395
00:31:51,001 --> 00:31:56,740
against the Corvair and in favor of legislation to establish uniform auto safety standards.

396
00:31:57,920 --> 00:32:03,540
One result was the creation of the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration, NHTSA,

397
00:32:03,541 --> 00:32:09,360
to set regulations for passenger vehicles, trucks, and school buses sold in the United States.

398
00:32:09,361 --> 00:32:17,120
The legislation that came about from these congressional hearings said to the auto industry,

399
00:32:17,920 --> 00:32:22,640
you cannot any longer make and market whatever you feel like,

400
00:32:23,080 --> 00:32:31,580
but there have to be federal safety standards to which you must comply in each vehicle that you intend to sell to the public.

401
00:32:32,840 --> 00:32:38,360
In 1966, the year of the Senate hearings, sales of Corvairs dropped dramatically.

402
00:32:39,360 --> 00:32:42,960
But the demand for smaller fuel-efficient vehicles continued to grow.

403
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:47,560
So in 1970, Ford introduced the Pinto,

404
00:32:48,340 --> 00:32:53,100
a car initially targeted to weigh 2,000 pounds and to cost $2,000.

405
00:32:54,700 --> 00:32:59,760
The Pinto was basically a scaled-down version of a regular-sized car.

406
00:33:00,700 --> 00:33:04,820
But in order to keep the cost low and the weight low,

407
00:33:04,821 --> 00:33:12,340
So the Pinto was rushed into production without paying much attention to the vulnerability of the fuel tank.

408
00:33:19,320 --> 00:33:25,700
Located three inches from the rear bumper, the Pinto fuel tank was easily ruptured in a low-speed rear collision.

409
00:33:26,600 --> 00:33:32,900
The gasoline filler tube, welded by screws to the fender but not to the tank, often fell out on impact.

410
00:33:34,820 --> 00:33:40,960
Protruding bolts and edges from the differential and rear suspension could puncture the fuel tank's thin sheet metal.

411
00:33:42,300 --> 00:33:44,520
The spilled gasoline could easily ignite.

412
00:33:45,860 --> 00:33:50,760
Also, the impact of a collision could jam the doors shut, trapping victims inside.

413
00:33:50,761 --> 00:33:59,240
This test by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration

414
00:33:59,241 --> 00:34:02,500
in which a Pinto was hit from behind at 35 miles per hour

415
00:34:02,501 --> 00:34:05,300
showed just how dangerous the car could be

416
00:34:05,301 --> 00:34:10,700
It was a total disaster in terms of an unsafe fuel tank

417
00:34:10,701 --> 00:34:14,600
an unsafe filler tube, minimal rear body structure

418
00:34:14,601 --> 00:34:16,100
doors that would jam

419
00:34:16,101 --> 00:34:19,900
everything was bad about the Pinto in a rear impact

420
00:34:19,901 --> 00:34:26,820
Horrifying burn accidents from rear-end collisions made the Pinto notorious.

421
00:34:28,480 --> 00:34:34,140
Following their own crash tests, NHTSA set tougher legal safety standards for rear-end collisions.

422
00:34:34,800 --> 00:34:39,920
In 1978, Ford recalled 1.4 million Pintos for safety improvements.

423
00:34:40,780 --> 00:34:44,440
But by that time, the Pinto's reputation was beyond repair.

424
00:34:44,441 --> 00:34:51,060
when you start producing something that millions of people are going to use

425
00:34:51,061 --> 00:34:56,760
the consumer has expectations and if you don't meet those expectations there is somebody else

426
00:34:56,761 --> 00:35:01,400
that is going to satisfy them and the consumer is going to forget your name very fast

427
00:35:01,401 --> 00:35:08,520
but the story of the pinto was also a matter of missed opportunity

428
00:35:08,521 --> 00:35:13,420
and Ford's early crash tests have prototype Pintos.

429
00:35:13,421 --> 00:35:17,960
Some were equipped with airbags and a plastic bladder liner inside the fuel tank.

430
00:35:19,340 --> 00:35:26,460
This is a fuel cell bladder, and right next to it, this is an actual Pinto fuel tank,

431
00:35:26,780 --> 00:35:31,640
and inside is such a bladder liner, a safety bladder liner.

432
00:35:31,641 --> 00:35:41,360
Now Ford Motor Company crash tested Pintos with a similar safety bladder liner inside the sheet metal fuel tank.

433
00:35:41,980 --> 00:35:49,100
And in their crash tests, even though the sheet metal fuel tank was punctured in what Ford said are puncture prone areas,

434
00:35:49,800 --> 00:35:54,660
thanks to the safety bladder liner inside the fuel tank, there was no leakage.

435
00:35:56,940 --> 00:36:01,200
However, both the airbags and the fuel cell bladder had been eliminated.

436
00:36:01,640 --> 00:36:05,000
to save money by the time the Pinto hit the highway.

437
00:36:06,480 --> 00:36:10,300
It would have had airbags. It would have had a safety fuel tank.

438
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:14,460
It would have been the safest small vehicle ever made.

439
00:36:14,461 --> 00:36:21,460
And it would have been a true engineering marvel rather than a profound engineering disaster.

440
00:36:21,461 --> 00:36:22,511
disaster.

441
00:36:22,512 --> 00:36:32,440
The current federal safety standard for fuel tank integrity went into effect in 1976.

442
00:36:33,660 --> 00:36:39,680
It requires cars to withstand a rear-end collision of 30 miles per hour without resulting in fuel

443
00:36:39,681 --> 00:36:45,340
leakage. Engineering Disasters 4 will return on Modern Marvels.

444
00:36:45,341 --> 00:36:54,040
Jet travel is a familiar feature of the world in which we live.

445
00:36:54,900 --> 00:37:00,600
But it was a barely imaginable luxury when the world's first scheduled commercial jet, the Comet,

446
00:37:01,020 --> 00:37:04,500
took off from England's Heathrow Airport in May of 1952.

447
00:37:06,680 --> 00:37:10,040
The Comet was a pioneer plane that changed the world.

448
00:37:10,880 --> 00:37:13,020
It was also a craft with a fatal flaw.

449
00:37:13,021 --> 00:37:20,020
Comet development began during the darkest days of World War II.

450
00:37:20,021 --> 00:37:25,020
As England struggled to survive the war, engineers at Britain's de Havilland Aircraft Company

451
00:37:25,021 --> 00:37:31,020
were designing a revolutionary plane for the peace to follow.

452
00:37:31,021 --> 00:37:36,020
Airline industry experts claimed that commercial propeller planes would never be supplanted by jet aircraft

453
00:37:36,021 --> 00:37:41,020
when the comet debuted in 1952.

454
00:37:41,021 --> 00:37:45,980
But by cutting air travel time in half and affording more comfort, the comet was a hit.

455
00:37:46,540 --> 00:37:51,240
And it became a symbol of national pride for a country so recently ravaged by war.

456
00:37:52,040 --> 00:37:57,420
The comet proved to the world that jet commercial aviation was the way to go.

457
00:37:57,660 --> 00:37:59,440
It was higher, faster, safer.

458
00:37:59,441 --> 00:38:03,250
So the passengers said, well, I can get there in half the time. It's smoother.

459
00:38:03,251 --> 00:38:05,400
I'm going above the weather. This is for me.

460
00:38:05,401 --> 00:38:12,280
Along with convenience, the Comet introduced far-reaching innovations for civil aircraft,

461
00:38:12,820 --> 00:38:15,460
including the now-standard swept-back wing design.

462
00:38:17,020 --> 00:38:21,560
It was the first commercial plane to utilize a rapid under-wing refueling system,

463
00:38:22,180 --> 00:38:26,240
and the first to bleed air from the engines to pressurize and heat the interior,

464
00:38:26,720 --> 00:38:28,680
a major advance from propeller planes.

465
00:38:29,820 --> 00:38:34,460
People had used furnaces and pumps to pressurize and heat the interior,

466
00:38:34,461 --> 00:38:38,380
and they were a source of carbon monoxide poisoning and accidents that had happened.

467
00:38:39,000 --> 00:38:41,860
And the Comet got around that by using bleed air from the jet engines.

468
00:38:42,340 --> 00:38:45,320
Now, of course, you say, well, that's obvious.

469
00:38:45,800 --> 00:38:48,360
But it wasn't. It was the first time it was done.

470
00:38:49,580 --> 00:38:52,540
The Comet's fuselage, composed of a metal alloy,

471
00:38:52,980 --> 00:38:56,960
was light enough to carry 36 passengers at 500 miles an hour,

472
00:38:57,440 --> 00:39:02,520
yet strong enough to keep the cabin comfortably pressurized, even at 40,000 feet.

473
00:39:04,460 --> 00:39:14,200
The plane's polished aluminum exterior and distinctive rectangular windows showed off de Havilland's unique sense of style, a flourish that would carry a cost.

474
00:39:14,600 --> 00:39:18,800
There used to be a saying at de Havilland, and if it doesn't look right, it isn't right.

475
00:39:19,320 --> 00:39:21,920
Well, the airplane looked right, and it was very right.

476
00:39:22,880 --> 00:39:27,780
For two years, the comet flew passenger routes throughout Europe, Africa, and Asia.

477
00:39:28,700 --> 00:39:32,860
But like its namesake, the comet's reign would be dazzling but brief.

478
00:39:34,460 --> 00:39:39,460
A 1953 crash on takeoff was blamed on pilot error.

479
00:39:39,461 --> 00:39:49,460
Then in the spring of 1954, two comets fell from the sky without warning over the Mediterranean Sea, killing all aboard.

480
00:39:49,461 --> 00:39:52,460
England went into shock.

481
00:39:52,461 --> 00:40:00,460
Winston Churchill called on the Royal Air Force to retrieve pieces of the comet from the sea and to search for clues to the mysterious disaster.

482
00:40:00,461 --> 00:40:03,460
The commercial comet fleet was grounded.

483
00:40:04,460 --> 00:40:09,460
To discover the cause of the crashes, engineers immersed a comet in a water tank

484
00:40:09,461 --> 00:40:12,460
and simulated the cyclical stresses of its performance in the air.

485
00:40:14,460 --> 00:40:18,460
The tests showed that the comet fuselage experienced metal fatigue

486
00:40:18,461 --> 00:40:23,460
at a rate relatively equal to the number of hours which had been flown by the two doomed jets.

487
00:40:25,460 --> 00:40:29,460
The comet's weakest stress points were at the corners of the plane's rectangular windows.

488
00:40:30,460 --> 00:40:37,420
When the windows cracked at 30,000 feet, the result was instant decompression, an explosion from within.

489
00:40:39,940 --> 00:40:43,740
Fatigue has to derive from some initiating anomaly.

490
00:40:44,120 --> 00:40:49,180
In the case of the comet, it was a very sharp corner in a window which created a stress concentration,

491
00:40:49,460 --> 00:40:54,580
which we know nowadays, sharp corners are an anathema to any structure carrying load.

492
00:40:57,680 --> 00:41:00,120
But the comet's journey was far from over.

493
00:41:00,460 --> 00:41:09,660
In 1958, de Havilland introduced the Comet 4, a bigger, stronger version of the original passenger plane, including reinforced oval windows.

494
00:41:10,780 --> 00:41:15,720
The Comet 4 promptly made headlines by becoming the first passenger jet to cross the Atlantic.

495
00:41:16,520 --> 00:41:21,880
Meanwhile, the lessons learned from the crash of the Comet 1 were shared with rival companies like Boeing,

496
00:41:22,320 --> 00:41:26,440
whose larger 707 jets would dominate the next era of commercial flight.

497
00:41:26,441 --> 00:41:33,900
The lessons of the Comet were being passed to the United States designers at all times.

498
00:41:33,980 --> 00:41:34,800
They were in contact.

499
00:41:35,440 --> 00:41:37,900
There was no commercial secrecy going involved.

500
00:41:37,901 --> 00:41:42,140
The aviation industry was too interested in getting the jet airliner right.

501
00:41:43,400 --> 00:41:48,920
The Comet 4 became a dependable carrier for the next 20 years on airlines throughout the world.

502
00:41:50,580 --> 00:41:52,800
Determined not to repeat the mistakes of the past,

503
00:41:52,801 --> 00:41:57,100
the plane set high standards for structural stability and piloting precision.

504
00:41:58,020 --> 00:42:00,600
You could touch down as lightly as a feather.

505
00:42:00,980 --> 00:42:04,340
It was very precise. It stopped well. It steered well.

506
00:42:04,780 --> 00:42:07,200
It was nice. It was just a lovely airplane.

507
00:42:08,620 --> 00:42:11,880
Today, at the Museum of Flight's hangar near Everett, Washington,

508
00:42:12,520 --> 00:42:15,300
Bob Hood is organizing a restoration of a Comet IV.

509
00:42:16,220 --> 00:42:19,540
It's a plane whose innovations are still utilized by jets today.

510
00:42:19,541 --> 00:42:22,260
from the redundancy of its hydraulic systems

511
00:42:22,261 --> 00:42:25,900
to its development of the now standard four-wheeled landing gear.

512
00:42:28,560 --> 00:42:32,660
I think we want people to know 50 years from now,

513
00:42:33,200 --> 00:42:34,940
at some undetermined point from now,

514
00:42:35,000 --> 00:42:36,840
what the first jet airliner was like.

515
00:42:40,160 --> 00:42:43,240
In an age where jet travel has become commonplace,

516
00:42:43,820 --> 00:42:47,180
the Comet holds a place in history as the plane that started it all.

517
00:42:47,181 --> 00:42:49,900
and the lessons imparted on its watch

518
00:42:49,901 --> 00:42:52,220
in triumph and in tragedy

519
00:42:52,221 --> 00:42:54,500
shaped the world we inhabit today.

520
00:42:55,200 --> 00:42:56,800
I would equate it with the Wright brothers

521
00:42:56,801 --> 00:42:58,660
at Kitty Hawk, quite frankly,

522
00:42:58,840 --> 00:43:01,820
because its implications were as much,

523
00:43:02,220 --> 00:43:03,020
if not more,

524
00:43:03,540 --> 00:43:04,840
in terms of transport

525
00:43:04,841 --> 00:43:06,620
and opening up the world.

526
00:43:09,760 --> 00:43:11,660
Planes and cars and bridges,

527
00:43:12,180 --> 00:43:14,660
high-rise hotels and high-flying spacecraft,

528
00:43:14,661 --> 00:43:18,040
all have drawn lessons from the failures of the past.

529
00:43:22,280 --> 00:43:27,340
Structural engineering is the art and science of molding materials

530
00:43:27,341 --> 00:43:32,540
we do not fully understand into shapes we cannot precisely analyze.

531
00:43:32,541 --> 00:43:36,020
To resist forces we cannot accurately predict,

532
00:43:36,580 --> 00:43:39,320
all in such a way that the society at large

533
00:43:39,321 --> 00:43:42,800
is given no reason to suspect the extent of our ignorance.

534
00:43:44,660 --> 00:43:48,660
Technological progress follows an unpredictable path,

535
00:43:48,661 --> 00:43:54,660
one which invariably strikes a balance between human ideals and human imperfection.

536
00:43:54,661 --> 00:44:02,660
We go and we look at structures, how they failed, why they failed, how they collapsed, and we improve.

537
00:44:02,661 --> 00:44:09,660
So, failures play an extremely important role in the progression of technology.

538
00:44:09,661 --> 00:44:15,160
July 2002.

539
00:44:15,580 --> 00:44:18,320
Work begins on a 700,000 square foot structure.

540
00:44:18,620 --> 00:44:19,940
And you're on the job.

541
00:44:20,160 --> 00:44:22,620
From nuts and bolts to basic engineering technology,

542
00:44:22,960 --> 00:44:26,240
take a firsthand look at every critical stage in design and construction

543
00:44:26,241 --> 00:44:27,700
of a modern office building.

544
00:44:27,940 --> 00:44:29,120
A modern Marvel special.

545
00:44:29,420 --> 00:44:30,520
Building a skyscraper.

546
00:44:30,980 --> 00:44:32,760
Tonight at 9 on the History Channel.

